{
  "id": "COMPOUND:301 - PROTEIN:970",
  "verbalization": "Ethoxzolamide appears to exert its effects on the body by increasing the occurrence of bleeding, nephrotoxicity, and gastrointestinal bleeding, which are mediated through the upregulation of Methazolamide and dichlorphenamide. Both Methazolamide and dichlorphenamide are known to target Penicillin-binding protein 1A, suggesting a potential mechanism where ethoxzolamide enhances the activity of these compounds in modulating Penicillin-binding protein 1A. This relationship is supported by the consistent evidence across multiple paths, indicating a shared pathway involving the upregulation of a compound that interacts with Penicillin-binding protein 1A. The integration of these findings highlights a possible role for ethoxzolamide in influencing the expression or function of Penicillin-binding protein 1A, potentially through the activation of downstream pathways that involve the upregulation of other compounds. The evidence suggests a plausible mechanism where ethoxzolamide may contribute to the modulation of Penicillin-binding protein 1A through the enhancement of the effects of Methazolamide and dichlorphenamide.",
